before:
A class selector has higher specificity than an element selector.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
p { color: purple; }
```
A paragraph with class="label" is teal even though the p rule comes later.

When matching rules have equal specificity, the later declaration wins.
```css
.label { color: teal; }
.label { color: brown; }
```
The second rule wins because these selectors have equal specificity.

An inherited value comes from a parent. A child’s own declared color takes precedence over an inherited color.
```css
body { color: navy; }
p { color: purple; }
```
The paragraph is purple; content without its own color can inherit navy.

after:
Specificity is how strongly a selector identifies a part. Here, a class selector beats a tag selector.

```css
.label {
  color: teal;
}
p {
  color: purple;
}
```

A paragraph with `class="label"` is teal. The class rule wins even though the paragraph rule comes later.

When two matching selectors have the same specificity, the later setting wins.

```css
.label {
  color: teal;
}
.label {
  color: brown;
}
```

Both selectors are the same. The paragraph is brown because that setting comes last.

Inheritance means getting a setting from a parent. A part’s own color rule wins over a color it could inherit.

```css
body {
  color: navy;
}
p {
  color: purple;
}
```

The paragraph is purple. A footer with no color of its own can inherit navy from the body.
